{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>YHWH, slow to anger.</b> One needs to know why he did not say before it all thirteen attributes that are stated in parashat Ki Tissa. And it may be explained according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory, in Rosh HaShanah, chapter 1, page 17, and this is their language: ״ה׳ ה׳״ — I am He before a person sins and I am He after a person sins and repents, end quote. And in parashat Tissa (Exodus 34:6) I have written the words of the Rosh, of blessed memory, on this and what occurred to me by way of explaining the matter. From this you have learned that the first attribute of mercy is before one sins; therefore here, where Moses needed the attribute that applies after the sin, for this reason he said only ״ה׳״ once." "<b>And according to the Ran</b> the Gaon, whose words the Tosafot (Rosh HaShanah 17b) and the Rosh brought — that the first ״ה׳״ is not counted among the attributes but rather informs who is the one calling. According to this there is a sound reason why Moses said here only one time ה׳, except that it is difficult how the Ran can disagree with the words of our Sages." "<b>And it is possible</b> to explain the matter according to the words of the Ran: that which they said, ״before one sins and after one sins,״ means that HaShem is merciful even after a person sins just as He was merciful before he sinned, and there is nothing novel about the mercy except after the sin. And according to this there is no difficulty from the Rosh's question, for he asked, ״before the sin, why is mercy needed?״ — for according to the truth, so it is. Also, he skipped three attributes: El, compassionate, and gracious, because these three attributes are needed by one who lacks deliverance from trouble, or one who lacks good, or one who is in pain, and now there was no need in the prayer for these attributes. And he began with slow to anger, to extend His anger even to the wicked, and as the Sages, of blessed memory, said (Sanhedrin 111a): when Moses ascended on high he found the Holy One, blessed be He, writing ״slow to anger״ etc. — see their words there. See, for it was ״slow to anger״ that he called upon. Also he said ״abundant kindness״ in order to tilt their judgment, as they said, of blessed memory (Rosh HaShanah 17b): ״abundant kindness״ — inclining toward kindness. And he did not mention ״truth״ nor ״preserving kindness to thousands״ because he did not need these attributes for the matter at hand. And he mentioned ״bearing iniquity and transgression״ because for them it was needed, and he did not mention ״and sin״ because they were not inadvertent sinners at that time but rather intentional. And he mentioned all the rest of the attributes because they are attributes relevant to the matter at hand."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:19.024369142Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}